            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div
    class="validbanner"
    role="note"
  >
    <span
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="ic"
    >
      !
    </span>
    <div>
      <h4>
        Read with caution — the response pattern tripped a validity check
      </h4>
      <p>
        This profile is shown in full, but one or more checks suggest the rankings may not reflect considered responses. Worth a conversation before the profile is used for coaching decisions. Not shown on the teacher-facing view.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Median time per item was 4.7s (under 8s).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <section
    aria-labelledby="ta-gate-h"
    class="gate"
    role="region"
  >
    <div
      class="lbl"
    >
      Priority conversation · Equity of treatment
    </div>
    <h3
      id="ta-gate-h"
    >
      This one is worth talking through before anything else.
    </h3>
    <p>
      Equity of treatment sits apart from the other six dimensions. The rest describe how a teacher works; this one describes who gets the benefit of it. A classroom can be well-run and still hand most of the attention, patience and benefit of the doubt to the same few children.
    </p>
    <p>
      On this profile it lands in Developing. That is a coaching conversation to schedule, not a performance record to file — the pattern below is specific enough to act on within a fortnight.
    </p>
    <div
      class="todo"
    >
      <b>
        What the responses showed
      </b>
      In the unit test scenario, this teacher ranked "Changes Kabir's mark on the spot to close the conversation and moves on, without checking any…" first. The framework places "Tells Kabir she'll review all similarly-marked answers before changing anything, then checks…" first — fixes the standard before acting on anyone's case — best equity outcome and process.
    </div>
    <div
      class="todo"
      style="margin-top: 10px;"
    >
      <b>
        One thing to try
      </b>
      For two weeks, track who you call on and who you follow up with. A 15-minute peer observation focused only on "who did I attend to" surfaces the pattern faster than any training input.
    </div>
  </section>
